#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wcvm - the WinCraft OS virtual machine of this folder.

Claude Code runs it for you (see CLAUDE.md); it works by hand too.

  ./wcvm doctor                     can this computer run the VM? what is missing?
  ./wcvm setup [--mem MB] [--cpus N] [--disk GB] [--graphics 3d|basic]
               [--display window|headless] [--firmware bios|uefi] [--res WxH]
               [--iso FILE | --version X.Y.Z]
                                    settings, WinCraft OS download, virtual disk
  ./wcvm install [--size MIB] [--name LABEL] [--window]
                                    install WinCraft OS on the blank virtual disk
  ./wcvm reinstall --yes [...]      erase the virtual disk and install again
  ./wcvm start [--window|--headless] [--live] [--wait]
  ./wcvm stop [--force]             shut down (--force: power off at once)
  ./wcvm restart [--wait]
  ./wcvm wait [shell|desktop]       until the VM's shell or desktop is up
  ./wcvm status [--json]
  ./wcvm screenshot [FILE.png]
  ./wcvm sh [--user] [--timeout S] COMMAND
                                    run a command in the VM as root, or as the
                                    person (--user) with their desktop's settings
  ./wcvm push FILE GUESTPATH        copy a file into the VM
  ./wcvm pull GUESTPATH FILE        copy a file out of it
  ./wcvm type TEXT | key COMBO... | click X Y [right] | dclick X Y | move X Y
  ./wcvm config [KEY=VALUE ...]     show or change the settings (next start)
  ./wcvm share                      mount the shared folder now (drive H:)
  ./wcvm snapshot [list] | save NAME | restore NAME --yes | delete NAME --yes
  ./wcvm update [check|install|previous]
                                    WinCraft OS's own updater, in the VM
  ./wcvm download [--version X.Y.Z] a WinCraft OS ISO, checked by its SHA-256
  ./wcvm logs [qemu|serial] [-n LINES]
                                    QEMU's output, or wcvm's shell sessions on the
                                    serial port (WinCraft starts up on the screen)
  ./wcvm destroy --yes [--all]      delete the VM (--all: the downloads too)

Everything lives in machine/ beside this file. QEMU is this computer's:
qemu-system-x86_64 and qemu-img from PATH, or $WCVM_QEMU and $WCVM_QEMU_IMG.

The VM is driven the way WinCraft OS's own development tools (tools/vm and
tools/qmp.py, github.com/PSthelyBlog/wincraft) drive it: QMP for the screen,
keyboard and mouse, and a root shell on the serial port, which the guest starts when the host asks for it with an SMBIOS
OEM string (rc.boot, images newer than 0.6.1).
"""
